00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:07.050
For most of this week, you've been using a encoder-decoder architecture for machine translation

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:12.130
where one RNN reads in a sentence and then a different one outputs a sentence.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:17.450
There's a modification to this called the attention model that makes all this work much

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.450
better.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.770
The attention algorithm, the attention idea has been one of the most influential ideas

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:23.770
in deep learning.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.650
Let's take a look at how that works.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:32.730
So given a very long French sentence like this, what we are asking this green encoder neural

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:37.510
network to do is to read in the whole sentence and then memorize the whole sentence and store

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:41.170
it in the activations conveyed here.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:47.130
And then for the purple network, the decoder network, to then generate the English translation.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.770
Jane went to Africa last September and joined the culture.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.850
She made wonderful people, she came by rating, but her wonderful trip was an attempt to

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.450
go to.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:01:00.490
Now the way a human translator would translate the sentence is not to first read the whole

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:05.210
French sentence and then memorize the whole thing and then regurgitate an English sentence

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:06.210
from scratch.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:12.850
Instead, what a human translator would do is read the first part of it, maybe generate

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:17.330
part of the translation, look at the second part, generate a few more words, look at

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.330
a few more words, generate a few more words and so on.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:25.050
And you kind of work part by part through the sentence because it's just really difficult

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:29.050
to memorize the whole long sentence like that.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:35.930
And so what you see for the encoder-decoder architecture above is that it works quite

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:41.210
well for short sentences, so it might achieve a relatively high blue score, but for very

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:47.450
long sentences, maybe longer than 30 or 40 words, the performance comes down.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:54.730
So the blue score might look like this as the sentence length varies and short sentences

00:01:54.730 --> 00:02:00.890
are just hard to translate, hard to get all the words right and long sentences it doesn't

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:05.090
do well on because it's just difficult to get the neural network to memorize a super

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.850
long sentence.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:13.050
So in this and the next video, you see the attention model which translates maybe a bit

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:17.650
more like humans might, looking at part of a sentence at a time.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:23.770
And with an attention model, machine translation systems performance can look like this because

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:29.130
by working one part of a sentence at a time, you don't see this huge dip which is really

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:34.250
measuring the ability of a neural network to memorize a long sentence which maybe isn't

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.770
what we most badly need a neural network to do.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:45.290
So in this video, I want to just give you some intuition about how attention works and

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:49.050
then we'll flesh out the details in the next video.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:56.730
The attention model was due to Zimitri Badanyu, Kiamkruk Cho, and Yoshibenjo.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:01.490
Even though it was originally developed for machine translation, it spread to many other

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.170
application areas as well.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:11.170
And this is really a very influential, I think very seminal paper in the deep learning literature.

